[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Stanford just built version control for AI coding agents. On a shared codebase benchmark, adding one supervisor on top took the pass rate from 28.8% to 54.7%. Nearly double from a single layer'

[00:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'sitting above the agents. It\'s called Shepherd. And here is how it works. Picture a coding agent 10 steps deep. It has written files, started a dev server, installed packages, warmed a'

[00:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'key-value cache. Then on step 10, it makes one bad edit and everything downstream breaks. You have two options today. Let it keep flailing, burning tokens on a run that\'s already dead.'

[00:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Or restart from step one and pay for every call again. And because the model is not deterministic, the restart won\'t even reproduce the run you are trying to fix. So, why can\'t you just jump back to step'

[00:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'eight? Because the only record you have is a message log. A transcript of what the agent said. The running state underneath is nowhere in that log. The open files, the child processes, the'

[01:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'temp directory, the warm cache. Git versions files. It cannot snapshot a running process or cold cache. Check out step eight and your files roll back, but the process is still living in step 10.'

[01:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'The state and the story have come apart. Shepherd records the whole run as a trace of typed events. And every interaction becomes a commit, exactly like it. Except this commit captures the agent process and suffices them together,'

[01:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'copy-on-write. Going back is a single call. You fork from any commit and continue from the exact state that existed at that moment. Not a rerun. A resume and snapshotting a whole run'

[01:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'sounds slow. It isn\'t. A fork takes 134 milliseconds, about five times faster than a Docker commit, and up to 374 times faster than copying a full file'

[01:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'system. And that number stays flat, whether the image is 40 megabytes or almost 6 gigabytes. Revert is the same, around 140 milliseconds. Because the prefix never changed, about'

[02:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '95% of the key-value cache is reused on replay. You are not paying to sync those tokens again. Once you can fork a running agent, a meta agent can sit on top and steer.'

[02:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Stanford showed three ways. One, runtime intervention. A supervisor watches two agents editing the same code base and steps in, injecting a note, handing off finished work, or discarding a branch that went'

[02:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'wrong. That is the 28 to 54 jump. Two, counterfactual optimization. Instead of rerunning an entire workflow to test a change, it forks at the first affected step and replace only the tail.'

[02:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'On a coding benchmark, it beat the previous method 51 to 40 at less wall clock time. Three, tree reinforcement learning. Fork four siblings at a time to score'

[02:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'each step on its own. That lifted a coding agent by 15%. But not everything can be undone. Files and sandbox changes revert cleanly. A database write needs a matching undo'

[03:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'step set up in advance. And a sent email or a real charge cannot be taken back at all. So, the supervisor\'s real job is to catch those before they fire. Shepherd is early and alpha, but they'

[03:20] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'prove the core correct in lean, and it wraps the sandboxes you already use. Git made file changes reversible. Shepherd does the same for a running agent. So, here is the real question.'

[03:32] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Would you let an automated supervisor auto revert your agent\'s mistakes? Or would you keep a hand on the wheel? Tell me below and subscribe for more.'
